The submission does not fully meet the generality criterion. Its custom JSON Schema reference rewriting depends on argument names.

In `_rewrite_refs`, keys in `_SCHEMA_VALUE_KEYWORDS` (`const`, `default`, `enum`, and `examples`) have their values left untouched at **every dictionary level**. Those strings are JSON Schema keywords in a schema object, but they can also be ordinary argument names inside `properties`.

For example, a JSON-schema-backed tool can legitimately use:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "default": {"$ref": "#/definitions/Value"}
  },
  "definitions": {
    "Value": {"type": "string"}
  }
}
```

`_ShownArgsSchema` removes `definitions` from the main schema and hoists its entries into Pydantic-managed definitions. It therefore needs to rewrite `#/definitions/Value`. However, when `_rewrite_refs` reaches the argument named `default`, it skips that argument’s schema entirely, leaving the old reference unchanged. The resulting schema has a dangling reference, or schema generation fails while resolving it.

Renaming that argument to an ordinary name allows the reference to be rewritten. Thus the per-call schema does not work independently of arbitrary argument names and caller-provided schema structure, even though the implementation has no hard-coded test tool names.

FAIL
